          %h2#InHomotopyTheory Cubical sets in homotopy theory
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"}> Daniel Kan
            ‘s early work on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            used cubical sets instead of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            = succeed "s:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy n-type
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} normalization of chains
                is essential
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+group"}> cubical group
                s are not automatically fibrant
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
                %a{:href => "#GeometricRealization"} geometric realization
                below) tends to have the wrong
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+n-type"} homotopy type
              %p
                for instance the geometric realization of the cubical set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                      %mo ×
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \times I
                has non-trivial
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+group"} homotopy group
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                given by the standard square, which is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible"} contractible
          %p
            It was then realized that none of these problems is shared by
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %ol
            %li
              %p Eilenberg and Mac Lane proved a normalisation theorem which may be found in Mac Lane’s book ‘Homology’.
            %li
              %p
                every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                is necessarily a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan complex
                and therefore fibrant in the standard
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+realization"} geometric realization
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+category"} cartesian monoidal
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo :
                      %mi SSet
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ←
                      %mi Top
                      %mo :
                      %mi S
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      |-| : SSet \stackrel{\leftarrow}{\to} Top : S(-)
              %p
                between
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SSet"} SSet
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
          %p These observations led to a widespread use of simplicial methods, while cubical methods coexisted only with a kind of underground existence, to this date.
          %p
            There is, however, a proof of parts of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            also for cubical sets: their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            is equivalent, after all, to the standard homotopy catgeory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Top"} Top
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
          %p
            Also, it turns out that the second and third of the above disadvantages of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+set"} cubical set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            can be dealt with to some extent.
          %ol
            %li
              %p The first problem can’t be avoided. See
              %p
                Rosa Antolini and Bert Wiest,
                %em The singular cubical set of a topological space
                , Mathematical Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society, 126 (1), (1999)
            %li
              %p
                The second problem is resolved, at least when restricting to
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-groupoid"} strict omega-groupoid
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} cubical sets with connection
              %p
                A. P. Tonks,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cubical groups which are Kan
            %li
              %p
                the third problem is due to the fact that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cube+category"} cube category
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"} test category
                but not a
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} with connection
                is a strict test category, as shown by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"} Georges Maltsiniotis
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Denis-Charles Cisinski
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} connection on a cubical set
                for details.
