              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sd
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sd
            is discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Jardine02"} Jardine 0, Section 5
            = succeed "-cube," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi sd
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sd X
            of a cubical set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is constructed from this naive subdivision of the
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a{:href => "#Jardine02"} Jardine’s lecture notes
            for details. There is a natural sequence of maps of cubical sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi sd
                    %mi n
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %mi sd
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots  \to sd^n X \to \cdots  \to sd X  \to X
          %p
            defined similar to its
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrant+replacement"} simplicial counterparts
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            be denoted as usual by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ex
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex X
            = succeed "-cubes" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ex
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex X
            are cubical maps from subdivision of the
            = succeed "-cube" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (similar to the definition of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibrant+replacement"} simplicial Ex-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ex
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Ex
                    %mn 2
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X  \to Ex X \to Ex^2 X  \to \cdots
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ex
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex^\infty X
            be the union of the latter maps (similar to simplicial
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Ex
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex^\infty
          %p
            %strong> question
            \: Is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ex
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex^\infty X
            a fibrant cubical set for any cubical set
            = succeed "?." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Recall that a cubical set is fibrant if any cubical horn has a filler (similar to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+complex"} Kan fibrant
            simplicial set has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/horn"} horn
            fillers). See also (
            %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"}> Cisinski 2006
            ) or
            %a{:href => "#JardineLecture"} Jardine’s lectures on cubical sets
            for definitions.
          %p
            The first question is probably not true in general, but if we consider cubical sets with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} connections
            in the sense of Brown-Higgins (we add some degeneracy maps to cubical sets), see e.g.
            %a{:href => "http://people.math.jussieu.fr/~maltsin/ps/cubique.ps"} Maltsiniotis paper
            then the cubical subdivision remains the same and the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ex
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex^\infty X
            is defined similarly. The question is whether
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Ex
                    %mn ∞
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ex^\infty X
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a cubical set with connections is fibrant.
            %strong Is it true?
          %h3#ModelCategoryStructureAndHomotopyTheory Model category structure and homotopy theory
          %p
            There is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski+model+structure"} Cisinski model structure
            on cubical sets with the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            as the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
            (
            %a{:href => "#Jardine02"}> Jardine 02
            ), which models
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-groupoids"} infinity-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
            for more information.
          %p
            In fact (
            %a{:href => "#Jardine02"}> Jardine 02, theorem 29, theorem 30
            ) gives an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            between the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            which, while not quite a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+an+adjunction"} counit
            being
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor
            it should indeed follow that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors"} derived functors
            of the adjunction exhibit the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localizations"} simplicial localizations
            of cubical sets equivalent to that of simplicial sets, hence make their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} equivalent
            (hence equivalent to
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
          %p
            This generalizes to a local
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+presheaves"} model structure on cubical presheaves
            over any
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            as the corresponding local
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+presheaves"} model structure on simplicial presheaves
          %p
            The category of cubical sets also admits a Joyal-type model structure, which admits a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joyal+model+structure"} Joyal model structure
            on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+for+cubical+quasicategories"} model structure for cubical quasicategories
            for more information.
          %h2#background Background
          %p
            The notion of cubical set and of homology theory and homotopy theory based on singular cubes goes way back in the literature. Serre’s work on spectral sequences and fibre spaces was based on cubes. Kan’s early work on combinatorial homotopy was based on cubes. However the category of cubical sets was found to have a major disadvantage compared with simplicial sets, in that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            in this category failed to have the correct
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
          %p Nonetheless cubical sets continued to have a kind of underground existence.
          %p
            Brown and Higgins introduced the extra structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+cubical+set"} connections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi i
                    %mo ±
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma^\pm_i
            on cubical sets, and included this structure into their cubical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} strict ∞-groupoids
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/crossed+complexes"} crossed complexes
            and for the applications to homotopy theory. For example these
            = succeed "-groupoids" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+elements"} thin elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ±
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mi j
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ±
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi i
                      %mo ±
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pm \epsilon_j x, \pm \Gamma^\pm_i
          %p The geometric realisation of cubical sets with connections, and the relation with cartesian products, has been analysed by Maltsiniotis in the paper referred to below.
          %p Nonetheless, the advantages of cubes are:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Easy notions of multiple compositions (compared with the globular pasting schemes); we refer to
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compositions+in+cubical+sets"} compositions in cubical sets
            %li
              %p
                Good notions of tensor product, because of the rule
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mi m
                        %mo ×
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mi n
                        %mo ≅
                        %msup
                          %mi I
                          %mrow
                            %mi m
                            %mo +
                            %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^m \times I^n \cong I^{m+n}
          %ul
            %li
              Al-Agl, F. A.,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
              %em Adv. Math.
              170~(1) (2002) 71–118.
          %p
            which gives a monoidal closed structure on cubical
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            = succeed "-categories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray+tensor+product"} Gray tensor product
            of 2-categories. This is also convenient in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+structure+on+C%2A-algebras"} homotopical structure on C*-algebras
          %p Cubical methods are a key feature in using higher homotopy groupoids to prove homotopy classification results.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %h3#general General
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicubical+sets"} bicubical sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+object"} cubical object
          %h3#applications_of_cubical_sets Applications of cubical sets
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
          %h4#in_higher_category_theory In higher category theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+groupoid+of+a+cubical+set+and+the+cubical+nerve+of+a+groupoid"} fundamental groupoid of a cubical set and the cubical nerve of a groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis+for+1-types"} homotopy hypothesis for 1-types
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+infinity-groupoid"} cubical infinity-groupoid
          %h3#theory_of_cubical_sets Theory of cubical sets
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cubes"} category of cubes
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+geometric+realisation"} cubical geometric realisation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubulation"} cubulation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+truncation%2C+skeleton%2C+and+co-skeleton"} cubical truncation, skeleton, and co-skeleton
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+a+cubical+Kan+complex"} homotopy groups of a cubical Kan complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+sets"} model structure on cubical sets
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cubical+presheaves"} model structure on cubical presheaves
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The original reference for cubical sets (based on the 1950 paper by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} Samuel Eilenberg
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+A.+Zilber"} J. A. Zilber
            on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+M.+Kan"}> Daniel M. Kan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abstract homotopy. I
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1073/pnas.41.12.1092"} doi
          %p
            Kan switched to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            in Part III of the series.
          %p Another early reference is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean-Pierre+Serre"}> Jean-Pierre Serre
              ,
              %em Homologie singulière des espaces fibrés
              , Ann.
              %p
                Math.
                %strong 54
                no.3 (1951), pp.425-505. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.college-de-france.fr/media/jean-pierre-serre/UPL7235285843586540944_Serre_The_se.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p General introductions of the cube category and of cubical sets are in
          %ul
            %li#JardineLecture
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rick+Jardine"}> Rick Jardine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cubical sets
                %a{:href => "https://www.uwo.ca/math/faculty/jardine/courses/fields/fields-01.pdf"} Fields Lectures on simplicial presheaves
                (unpublished).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Sjoerd+Crans"}> Sjoerd Crans
                , section 2 of
                %em
                  Pasting schemes for the monoidal biclosed structure on
                  = succeed "-" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ω
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi Cat
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{Cat}
                (
                %a{:href => "http://crans.fol.nl/papers/thten.html"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://crans.fol.nl/papers/thten.ps.gz"} ps
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/thten.pdf", :title => ""} pdf
          %p
            The
            %strong cubical identities
            satisfied by a cubical set are given there in proposition 2.8 on p. 9.
          %p
            Cubical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Massey, W. S.,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Singular homology theory
              = succeed "," do
                %em Graduate Texts in Mathematics
          %p
            An axiomatization of cubical sets in
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+set+theory"} constructive set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            (with the aim of building models of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %ul
            %li#BezemCoquandHuber13
              %p
                Marc Bezem,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simon+Huber"} Simon Huber
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A model of type theory in cubical sets
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2014/4628/"} web
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://drops.dagstuhl.de/opus/volltexte/2014/4628/pdf/7.pdf"} pdf
            %li#KaposiAltenkirch14
              %p
                Ambrus Kaposi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thorsten+Altenkirch"} Thorsten Altenkirch
                %em A syntax for cubical type theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://mazzo.li/dump/aim-kaposi-pres.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#Docherty14
              %p
                Simon Docherty,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A model of type theory in cubical sets with connection
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.illc.uva.nl/Research/Publications/Reports/MoL-2014-12.text.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"}> Dan Licata
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Guillaume+Brunerie"} Guillaume Brunerie
              %em A cubical approach to synthetic homotopy theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/lb15cubicalsynth/lb15cubicalsynth.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            For more on this see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+category+theory+and+type+theory"} relation between category theory and type theory
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            \/
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure on cubical sets is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Jardine02
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rick+Jardine"}> Rick Jardine
              ,
              %em Cubical homotopy theory: a beginning
              (2002) (
              %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20201229182153/http://hopf.math.purdue.edu/Jardine/cubical2.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            The fact that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+object"} exponential object
            of two fibrant cubical sets is again fibrant follows from remark 8.4.33 in
          %ul
            %li#Cisinski06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/joyalscatlab/published/Les+pr%C3%A9faisceaux+comme+type+d%27homotopie"} Les préfaisceaux comme type d'homotopie
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mathematik.uni-regensburg.de/cisinski/ast.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            in the context of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski+model+structures"} Cisinski model structures
          %p
            Finally cubical sets as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalence"} univalence
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Cisinski14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              %em Univalent universes for elegant models of homotopy types
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1406.0058"}> arXiv:1406.0058
              )
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+category"} strict test category
            nature of cubical sets with connection is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em La catégorie cubique avec connexions est une catégorie test stricte
          %p There is also the old work
          %ul
            %li
              Victor Gugenheim,
              %em On supercomplexes
              Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 85 (1957), 35–51
              %a{:href => "https://www.ams.org/journals/tran/1957-085-01/S0002-9947-1957-0086299-1/S0002-9947-1957-0086299-1.pdf"} PDF
          %p in which “supercomplexes” are discussed, that combine simplicial sets and cubical sets (def 5). There are functors from simplicial sets to supercomplexes (after Defn 5) and, implicitly, from supercomplexes to cubical sets (in Appendix II). This was written in 1956, long before people were thinking as formally as nowadays and long before Quillen model theory, but a comparison of the homotopy categories might be in there.
          %p A discussion of cubical sets and normal forms in several cases is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marco+Grandis"}> Marco Grandis
              , and Mauri, L. Cubical sets and their site,
              %em Theory Applic. Categories
              {11} (2003) 185–201.
          %p
            Cubical sets as models for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} strict ∞-groupoids
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              , P. Higgins,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  The equivalence of
                  = succeed "-groupoids" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ω
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
                  = succeed "-complexes" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi T
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://archive.numdam.org/ARCHIVE/CTGDC/CTGDC_1981__22_4/CTGDC_1981__22_4_349_0/CTGDC_1981__22_4_349_0.pdf"} pdf
          %p Their use for monoidal closed structures and homotopy classification is given in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"} Ronnie Brown
              and P. Higgins, Tensor products and homotopies for
              = succeed "-groupoids" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ω
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
              %em J. Pure Appl. Algebra
              47 (1987) 1–33.
          %p and are essential in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ronnie+Brown"}> Ronnie Brown
              , Philip J. Higgins and Rafael Sivera,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nonabelian+Algebraic+Topology"} Nonabelian Algebraic Topology
              EMS Tracts in Math vol 15, 703 pages, August, 2011. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.bangor.ac.uk/~mas010/nonab-a-t.html"}> web
              ).
          %p
            Essential subtoposes of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo □
                        %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\Box^{op}}
            are discussed in the context of Lawvere’s dialectical theory of dimension in
          %ul
            %li#KRRZ10
              C. Kennett,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} E. Riehl
              %em Levels in the toposes of simplicial sets and cubical sets
              , JPAA
              %strong 215
              no.5 (2011) pp.949-961. (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1003.5944"}> preprint
              )
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            model of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+category"} Quillen model category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+cube+category"} cartesian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+mathematics"} classically
            presents the usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spaces"} spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Evan+Cavallo"} Evan Cavallo
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thierry+Coquand"} Thierry Coquand
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"} Emily Riehl
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Sattler"} Christian Sattler
              %em The equivariant model structure on cartesian cubical sets
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.18497"}> arXiv:2406.18497
              )
          %p Varieties of cubical sets are discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrik+Buchholtz"}> Ulrik Buchholtz
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Morehouse"} Edward Morehouse
              = succeed "." do
                %em Varieties of Cubical Sets
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+H%C3%B6fner"} Peter Höfner
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Damien+Pous"} Damien Pous
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Struth"} Georg Struth
              (eds)
              = succeed "." do
                %em Relational and Algebraic Methods in Computer Science
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-57418-9_5"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-57418-9_5
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.08189"} arXiv:1701.08189
        .revisedby
